

====================================================================
AVONE LAUNCH COMMANDS 
====================================================================


====================================================================
MAIN CONTROL BRINGUP
====================================================================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_control_bringup.launch.py

What this launches with individual commands:
    - Generates robot_description from xacro (URDF)
    - Publishes TF and joint states via robot_state_publisher
    - Starts ros2_control and loads the Ackermann steering controller
    - Spawns joint_state_broadcaster, then steering controller (sequenced)
    - Relays controller odometry TF into /tf
    - Starts cmd_vel filtering
    - Launches teleop control for manual testing
    - Optionally launches RViz after controllers are active






=====================
GPS BRINGUP
=====================

Command:
 ros2 launch avone avone_gps_bringup.launch.py

What this launches:
    - Starts the NMEA GPS serial driver
    - Publishes GPS fixes from a USB serial device
    - Sets the GPS frame for localisation integration
    - Converts GPS heading output into an IMU-compatible message
    - Provides heading data for robot_localization and navigation

Individual Commdands:

• GPS serial driver (NMEA)
  - Reads GPS data from USB serial
  - Publishes NavSatFix messages
  - Sets GPS frame for localisation

  Command:
    ros2 run nmea_navsat_driver nmea_serial_driver \
      --ros-args \
      -p port:=/dev/ttyUSB1 \
      -p baud:=115200 \
      -p frame_id:=gps_link1


• GPS heading → IMU conversion
  - Converts GPS heading output into IMU-compatible data
  - Used as yaw input for robot_localization

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_localisation heading_to_imu

=====================
IMU BRINGUP
=====================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_imu_bringup.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Starts the VectorNav IMU interface node
  - Initializes IMU communication and data publishing
  - Publishes IMU orientation, angular velocity, and acceleration data
  - Provides IMU data for localisation and state estimation
  - (Optional) Static transform support for IMU frame alignment

Individual Commands:

• VectorNav IMU bringup
  - Starts IMU communication
  - Publishes sensor_msgs/Imu data
  - Used as the primary IMU source for localisation

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_localisation imu_start \
      --ros-args \
      -p use_sim_time:=false

=====================
LIDAR BRINGUP
=====================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_lidar_bringup.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Starts the Quanergy LiDAR client
  - Connects to the LiDAR over Ethernet
  - Publishes LiDAR scan and point cloud data
  - Publishes a static transform for the LiDAR frame
  - Starts collision detection using LiDAR data

Individual Commands:

• Quanergy LiDAR client
  - Connects to the Quanergy LiDAR over Ethernet
  - Receives raw LiDAR data
  - Publishes point cloud and scan topics

  Command:
    ros2 launch quanergy_client_ros client.launch.py \
      host:=192.168.1.57


• Static transform: laser_frame → quanergy
  - Defines fixed transform between vehicle frame and LiDAR frame
  - Used for correct TF alignment of LiDAR data

  Command:
    ros2 run tf2_ros static_transform_publisher \
      0 0 0 \
      0 0 0 \
      laser_frame quanergy


• Collision detection
  - Monitors LiDAR data for front on obsticals
  - Publishes collision CAN messaged, causing fault state
  - Used as a safety layer during autonomous operation

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_nav collision_detection

=====================
LOCALISATION BRINGUP
=====================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_localisation_bringup.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Starts the local (odom-frame) EKF for vehicle state estimation
  - Starts the global (map-frame) EKF for fused global localisation
  - Launches navsat_transform to fuse GPS data into the localisation stack
  - Provides odometry and pose outputs for navigation and control
  - Establishes the full robot_localization pipeline (local + global)

Individual Commands:

• Local EKF (odom frame)
  - Fuses IMU, wheel odometry, and local sensors
  - Publishes locally consistent odometry
  - Used for control and short-horizon navigation

  Command:
    ros2 launch avone_localisation local_ekf.launch.py


• Global EKF (map frame)
  - Fuses local odometry with global corrections
  - Provides globally referenced pose estimates
  - Used for navigation, mapping, and logging

  Command:
    ros2 launch avone_localisation global_ekf.launch.py


• NavSat transform
  - Converts GPS (NavSatFix) into a local Cartesian frame
  - Aligns GPS data with IMU heading and odometry
  - Feeds transformed GPS data into the global EKF

  Command:
    ros2 launch avone_localisation navsat_transform.launch.py


--------------------------------------------------------------------

Optional: Single-EKF Localisation (All Sensors Fused)

• AV.ONE single EKF
  - Fuses IMU, wheel odometry, and GPS in a single filter
  - Publishes a unified filtered odometry output
  - Used for early bringup, testing, or simplified deployments
  - Not intended for final competition configuration

  Command:
    ros2 launch avone_localisation avone_ekf.launch.py



=====================
NAV2 BRINGUP
=====================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_nav2_bringup.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Starts the AV.ONE Nav2 navigation stack
  - Launches the core Nav2 lifecycle nodes and planners
  - Enables path planning and path following
  - Provides autonomous navigation capability
  - Starts a Nav2 cancellation watchdog for safety and recovery

Individual Commands:

• Nav2 cancel watchdog
  - Monitors navigation state
  - Listens for CAN messages triggered by RC to start or cancel goal
  - Used as a safety and recovery mechanism

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_nav nav2_cancel.py


• Nav2 core stack
  - Starts Nav2 lifecycle manager and navigation servers
  - Includes planner, controller, and behavior tree execution
  - Provides autonomous path planning and tracking

  Command:
    ros2 launch avone_nav nav2.launch.py


=====================
NUC BOOT BRINGUP
=====================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_nuc_boot.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Starts the CAN DBC bridge for vehicle communication
  - Decodes CAN messages using the AV.ONE DBC file
  - Starts the LED matrix drive-state controller
  - Publishes system heartbeat messages from the NUC
  - Monitors sensor timeouts and communication health
  - Intended to run automatically on NUC boot

Individual Commands:

• DBC CAN bridge
  - Bridges CAN traffic into ROS topics
  - Decodes messages using the AV.ONE DBC file
  - Provides vehicle state data to the autonomy stack

  Command:
    ros2 run can_simulation test_cantoros \
      --ros-args \
      -p dbc_file:=/home/avone/NUTEAMSGIT/NUCAN/DBC\ Files/AV1.dbc \
      -p dbc_path:=/home/avone/NUTEAMSGIT/NUCAN/DBC\ Files/AV1.dbc


• Drive-state LED matrix controller
  - Controls the external LED matrix
  - Displays current AV state (READY, DRIVING, FAULT, etc.)
  - Used for scrutineering and test-day visibility

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_utils drive_state_led


• NUC heartbeat publisher
  - Publishes periodic heartbeat messages
  - Indicates that the NUC and ROS stack are alive
  - Used by safety and watchdog systems

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_utils ros_cmd_heartbeat


• Sensor timeout monitor
  - Monitors critical sensor topics
  - Detects missing or stale sensor data
  - Triggers fault handling on timeout conditions

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_utils sensor_timeout


===============================
ROBOT STATE PUBLISHER TEST
===============================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_rsp.launch.py use_sim_time:=false

What this launches:
  - Generates robot_description from description/robot.urdf.xacro in the avone package
  - Starts robot_state_publisher to publish the full TF tree from the URDF
  - Starts joint_state_publisher_gui so you can manually move joints for RViz checks
  - Starts RViz2 using the saved config: config/view_avone.rviz
  - Intended for desktop validation only, no Gazebo, no ros2_control

Notes:
  - This launch file declares use_sim_time but currently the robot_state_publisher node is hard set to use_sim_time:=True
  - If you run with use_sim_time:=true, you must have /clock being published, otherwise TF can appear frozen in RViz
  - If you want a clean desktop test, run with use_sim_time:=false and also set robot_state_publisher to use that argument

Individual Commands:

• Generate robot_description from Xacro
  - Expands the AV.ONE xacro into a URDF string
  - Used internally by robot_state_publisher

  Command:
    ros2 run xacro xacro \
      $(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/description/robot.urdf.xacro

  Example (save to a file):
    ros2 run xacro xacro \
      $(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/description/robot.urdf.xacro \
      > /tmp/avone.urdf


• Robot State Publisher
  - Publishes TF transforms from the URDF model
  - Uses the robot_description parameter
  - This is what makes the TF tree appear in RViz

  Command:
    ros2 run robot_state_publisher robot_state_publisher \
      --ros-args \
      -p use_sim_time:=false \
      -p robot_description:="$(ros2 run xacro xacro $(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/description/robot.urdf.xacro)"


• Joint State Publisher GUI
  - Provides a slider GUI to move joints manually
  - Publishes /joint_states which drives TF updates in RViz

  Command:
    ros2 run joint_state_publisher_gui joint_state_publisher_gui


• RViz2 (AV.ONE config)
  - Opens RViz using the saved config in the avone package
  - Typically preloads TF, RobotModel, and fixed frame settings

  Command:
    ros2 run rviz2 rviz2 \


• Optional: Static map -> odom transform
  - Publishes a fixed transform between map and odom
  - Useful if your RViz config expects a map fixed frame but you do not have localisation running

  Command:
    ros2 run tf2_ros static_transform_publisher \
      0 0 0 0 0 0 map odom


Quick sanity checks:

• Check TF is publishing:
  Command:
    ros2 topic echo /tf --once

• Check joint states are publishing:
  Command:
    ros2 topic echo /joint_states --once

• If RViz looks frozen and you used sim time:
  Command:
    ros2 topic echo /clock --once


=========================
AV.ONE SIMULATION BRINGUP
=========================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_sim.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Sets Gazebo model search paths (IGN_GAZEBO_RESOURCE_PATH, GZ_SIM_RESOURCE_PATH)
  - Publishes robot_description from robot.urdf.xacro
  - Starts robot_state_publisher (TF from URDF)
  - Launches Gazebo Sim with the selected world
  - Spawns the AV.ONE robot entity into the world
  - Starts ros2_control + spawns controllers (joint_state_broadcaster, ackermann_steering_controller)
  - Bridges Gazebo topics into ROS 2 (clock, TF, IMU, lidar, cameras, navsat)
  - Starts robot_localization (EKF + navsat_transform) for simulated localisation
  - Relays /imu to /imu_gps and /imu/data
  - Starts joystick teleop (joy + teleop_twist_joy + cmd_vel_filter)
  - Optionally starts RViz after a delay

Notes:
  - use_sim_time should be true when using Gazebo
  - Default world:
      $(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/worlds/smalltrack.world
  - Robot entity name:
      my_robot

Individual Commands:

• Gazebo model paths
  Command:
    export IGN_GAZEBO_RESOURCE_PATH=$(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/worlds/models
    export GZ_SIM_RESOURCE_PATH=$(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/worlds/models


• Gazebo world
  Command:
    ros2 launch ros_gz_sim gz_sim.launch.py \
      gz_args:="-r -v1 $(ros2 pkg prefix avone)/share/avone/worlds/smalltrack.world"


• Robot state publisher (URDF TF)
  Command:
    ros2 run robot_state_publisher robot_state_publisher \
      --ros-args \
      -p use_sim_time:=true


• Spawn robot (my_robot)
  Command:
    ros2 run ros_gz_sim create \
      -entity my_robot \
      -topic robot_description


• ROS <-> Gazebo bridge
  - Bridges clock, TF, cmd_vel, joint_states, and sensors into ROS 2
  Command:
    ros2 run ros_gz_bridge parameter_bridge <bridge_args...>


• ros2_control + controllers
  Command:
    ros2 run controller_manager ros2_control_node

  Command:
    ros2 run controller_manager spawner joint_state_broadcaster

  Command:
    ros2 run controller_manager spawner ackermann_steering_controller


• Localisation (sim)
  Command:
    ros2 run robot_localization ekf_node

  Command:
    ros2 run robot_localization navsat_transform_node


• IMU relays
  Command:
    ros2 run topic_tools relay /imu /imu_gps

  Command:
    ros2 run topic_tools relay /imu /imu/data


• Teleop + cmd_vel filter
  Command:
    ros2 run joy joy_node

  Command:
    ros2 run teleop_twist_joy teleop_node

  Command:
    ros2 run avone_utils cmd_vel_filter


• RViz2
  Command:
    ros2 run rviz2 rviz2


=========================
AV.ONE TEST SIM BRINGUP
=========================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone avone_test_sim.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Launches a minimal Gazebo sim (empty world)
  - Generates robot_description from robot.urdf.xacro
  - Starts robot_state_publisher (TF from URDF)
  - Spawns the robot into Gazebo from robot_description

Notes:
  - This is the simplest "does the URDF spawn" test
  - No ros2_control, no controllers, no bridges, no localisation, no sensors
  - use_sim_time should be true when using Gazebo

Individual Commands:

• Gazebo (empty world)
  Command:
    ros2 launch ros_gz_sim gz_sim.launch.py \
      gz_args:="-r -v1 /home/avone/avone_ws/src/avone/worlds/empty.sdf"


• Robot state publisher (URDF TF)
  Command:
    ros2 run robot_state_publisher robot_state_publisher


• Spawn robot into Gazebo
  - Spawns entity name: my_robot
  - Uses robot_description topic

  Command:
    ros2 run ros_gz_sim create \
      -entity my_robot \
      -topic robot_description \
      -x 0.0 -y 0.0 -z 0.0



===============================
AV.ONE SINGLE EKF LOCALISATION
===============================

Command:
  ros2 launch avone_localisation avone_ekf.launch.py

What this launches:
  - Starts a single robot_localization EKF (ekf_node)
  - Loads parameters from:
      avone_localisation/config/avone_ekf.yaml
  - Publishes filtered odometry on:
      /odometry/avone

Notes:
  - use_sim_time is a launch argument (default true)
  - Sensor inputs (IMU, wheel odom, GPS, etc.) are configured inside the YAML

Individual Commands:

• EKF (robot_localization)
  - Fuses sensor inputs into a filtered odometry estimate
  - Remaps output odometry to /odometry/avone

  Command:
    ros2 run robot_localization ekf_node \
      --ros-args \
      -p use_sim_time:=true \
      --params-file $(ros2 pkg prefix avone_localisation)/share/avone_localisation/config/avone_ekf.yaml \
      -r odometry/filtered:=/odometry/avone


